  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\n   The central idea is to develop an optimization algorithm for minimizing the AC1 upper bound by leveraging the evaluator's constraint structure. The key insight is that the AC1 upper bound is defined by a ratio of the maximum convolution value to the square of the sum of sequence coefficients, creating a nonconvex optimization problem. We address this by combining several strategies:\n   - Initializations: Use multiple starting points including the parent sequence, uniform distribution, and periodic patterns to ensure diversity.\n   - Iterative refinement: Apply projected gradient steps with adaptive time allocation using the $lp_optimize$ algorithm.\n   - Dimension extension: Parameterize search across different sequence lengths to escape local optima.\n   - Efficient polishing: Coordinate and pair optimizations converge candidates toward optimal structures.\n   This approach improves upon the previous formulation by focusing on time efficiency while maintaining mathematical rigor, and achieves lower upper bounds by systematically exploring the search space.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n   - Input: Sequence length $n$, initial candidate sequence $f_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$\n   - Output: Sequence $f \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ with $\\sum f_i \\ge 0.01$ and $0 \\le f_i \\le 1000$\n   - Objective: Minimize $2 \\cdot n \\cdot \\max(\\text{convolve}(f, \\text{full})) / (\\sum f)^2$\n   - Constraints: \n     - Nonnegativity: $f_i \\ge 0 \\forall i$\n     - Boundedness: $0 \\le f_i \\le 1000 \\forall i$\n     - Sum constraint: $\\sum f_i \\ge 0.01$\n   - Formulations:\n     - For length $n$ sequences, build constraint matrix $A$ where $A[j:j+n,j] = f$\n     - Solve linear program: $\\min -\\sum x_i$ s.t.$A^T x \\le \\max(\\text{convolve}(f,\\text{full}))$\n     - Update direction $g$ based on feasible solution\n     - Normalize direction to maintain gradient descent properties\n     - For length extension, perform interpolation and resampling\n   - Properties: The optimization is exact and preserves feasible candidates throughout iterations\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   - Initialization: \n     - Use $3$ initializations: \n       1. Parent sequence if available\n       2. Uniform sequence (all ones)\n       3. Periodic function: $0.3 + 0.7\\cos(\\pi i/n)^2$\n     - For each initialization, treat as independent sequence\n   - Iteration loop:\n     - For each initialization, set candidate sequence\n     - Compute reference convolve value\n     - Build optimization matrix $A$ with dimensions $(2n-1) \\times n$\n     - Solve linear program with solver tolerance $\\epsilon = 1e-12$\n     - Compute $\\sum g_i$ and normalize direction\n     - Apply update $f \\leftarrow (1-t) \\cdot f + t \\cdot g$ with adaptive $t$\n     - Clip updated coefficients to bounds\n     - Compute new score immediately post-update\n     - Track best found solution separately\n   - Coordinate optimization polishing:\n     - For best candidate, apply coordinate descent with random order\n     - For each coordinate, build influence vector from current $\\text{convolve}(f)$\n     - Perform golden-section search along coordinate axis\n     - Update coordinate with minimum observed score\n   - Dimension extension:\n     - After primary search, extend to lengths $\\{256, 512\\}$\n     - Use linear interpolation from best across dimensions\n     - Re-run initial iterations on extended candidates\n     - Apply fast coordinate refinement\n   - Deployment:\n     - Primary search $45\\%$ of budget\n     - Polishing $25\\%$ of budget\n     - Extension $30\\%$ of budget\n     - Return best scored valid sequence\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n   - Standard configuration:\n     - Initial $t$-value: $0.3$\n     - $t$-adaptation: $\\times 1.1$ increase, $\\times 0.8$ decrease\n     - $LP$ time limit: $45\\%$ of remaining budget\n     - Coordinate descent: $25\\%$ of budget\n     - Extension to $256$: $23\\%$ remaining time\n     - Extension to $512$: $23\\%$ remaining time\n   - Key variations:\n     - Use stochastic rounding instead of gradient stepping\n     - Incorporate sample sets for $cosine$, $sinc$, and $ramped sine$ periodic functions\n     - Apply dimension boosting to $128$, $256$, $512$\n     - Implement sparse-formulation convolve when $n > 512$\n     - Use barrier method with self-dual embedding for $LP$ solutions\n   - Triggered settings:\n     - When $score < 1.4$: tighten $t$ constraints\n     - When $dim = 384$: apply dimension jumping instead of polishing\n     - When $\\sum f_i < 0.01$: recover via coordinate forcing\n     - When $n > 1024$: switch to boundary optimization\n\n5. Computational budget and robustness\n   - Resource estimation:\n     - $LP$ for $512$ dimension: $\\approx 300$s\n     - Coordinate descent for $512$: $\\approx 400$s\n     - Extension search: $\\approx 200$s\n     - Total estimate: $900 \\pm 30\\%$s\n   - Time management:\n     - Use soft deadlines at $33\\%$, $66\\%$, $100\\%$ budget points\n     - Monitor elapsed time within expensive operators\n     - Save best candidate after every $10$ iterations\n   - Failure handling:\n     - Solver failures: use fallback initialization\n     - Invalid outputs: catch $np.inf$ or $nan$ scores\n     - Time overruns: reduce dimension search range\n     - Empty results: use $L^2$ projection from uniform sequence\n   - Construction fallback:\n     - If value becomes $nan$: reset to bounds-checked uniform\n     - If sum $< 0.01$: add $0.001$ to all coefficients\n     - If dimensions exceed $2048$: truncate to nearest available size\n     - Always return $n \\ge 128$ sequences\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n   - Check sequence:\n     - All values $\\in [0, 1000]$\n     - Sum $|\\sum f_i| \\ge 0.01$\n     - Finite-valued non $nan$\n   - Execution checks:\n     - Verify final candidate score against $\\le 1.5027\\text{max}$ criteria\n     - Re-evaluate via $evaluate\\_sequence$ for exact bounds\n     - Maintain separate tracking of best candidate during iterations\n   - Success metrics:\n     - Lowered AC1 upper bound compared to parent ($1.5095$)\n     - Mathematical consistency with evaluator\n     - Implementation resource compliance ($\\le 1300$s)\n   - Special checks:\n     - Zero-convolution candidate: ensure nontrivial entries\n     - Short sequence handling: $n \\to \\infty$ behavior\n     - Floating point safety: tolerance $\\epsilon = 1e-14$",
